    "reducedMotion": "No animation loop is started: one frame is drawn when the context is built, and one more after each move and each resize, so the field is a still image rather than a drifting one. The handoff still moves the canvas — that is a DOM relocation, not motion — but the FLIP tween and the `uHandoff` surge are both skipped, and the drawing buffer is resized immediately instead of on a completion that will never fire. `prefers-reduced-motion` is read by the component through `useReducedMotion`, never inferred from the page.",
    "cleanup": "`disposeRenderer` stops the animation loop before releasing the context, so a queued frame cannot render into a lost one and throw out of the cleanup; it then calls `dispose()`, `forceContextLoss()` and zeroes the canvas dimensions to release the backing store immediately. The `ResizeObserver` is disconnected, both the canvas transform tweens and the `uHandoff` tween are killed, the material and geometry are disposed, and the canvas element is removed by the same effect that appended it. The flight effect's own cleanup kills its timeline and restores the destination slot's original `overflow`, so an unmount mid-flight cannot leave a slot permanently unclipped.",
    "acceptance": "Watch one bright fold of the field and click a nav item: the same fold is still there when the canvas lands, offset only by the time the flight took. Nothing blanks, blinks, or restarts. The canvas visibly changes proportion in flight — wide banner to tall column — with the dither cells stretching along with it, then snapping to square at the end when the buffer is finally resized. Click twice in quick succession and the second flight starts from wherever the canvas is in the air rather than jumping to the first destination. In React DevTools the canvas element has no owner. Under `reactStrictMode` in development the preview renders on first load, not blank. With reduced motion the field is a still image and the canvas relocates without a flight.",
