    "reducedMotion": "`useSpringEasing` recomputes with `bounce: 0` and `velocity: 0`, forcing the damping ratio to exactly 1. That removes the oscillation — the trigger for vestibular discomfort — while keeping the stiffness and therefore the pace, so the motion stays the same length and simply arrives once instead of settling. It is not shortened to zero: the curve is still a real ease, just monotonic, and the demo's peak readout drops to 100% to show it.",
    "cleanup": "Nothing to clean up: `springToLinear` is a pure function and `useSpringEasing` only calls it inside `useMemo`. No timers, listeners, or animation frames are created. The reduced-motion subscription belongs to `useReducedMotion`, which removes its own media-query listener on unmount.",
    "acceptance": "Press Run. The three squares cross their tracks and each one visibly passes its end position and settles back — Loose the most, Snap barely. The plotted curve beside each row rises above the dashed 100% line by the amount stated in the readout. Scroll or drag hard during the run and the motion does not hitch, because it is not on the main thread. Read the emitted string: it starts at 0, contains values greater than 1, and ends at exactly 1. Turn reduced motion on and re-run: every curve is monotonic, no square passes its end position, every peak reads 100%, and the durations are still hundreds of milliseconds rather than zero.",
