    "reducedMotion": "No rAF subscription is opened and one still frame is drawn with `uForm` forced to 1 and the turntable at 0.6 rad — a three-quarter view of the fully assembled shell with its rings at full opacity, not the unformed cloud. The shell, the density, the depth cue from perspective point sizing and the measured rings are all present; the assembly, the breathing and the rotation are what stop. A resize redraws the same still.",
    "cleanup": "The source object is disposed immediately after sampling, before the renderer even exists. On unmount the stage ref is nulled so an in-flight frame cannot draw into a dying context, `disposeObject(scene)` disposes the point geometry, the ring geometry and their materials, the point material and the ring material are disposed explicitly as well, then `disposeRenderer` performs `dispose()` + `forceContextLoss()` + a zeroed drawing buffer — `dispose()` alone leaves the context alive — and the canvas node is removed. `useRaf`, `useInView`, `useVisibility` and `useResizeObserver` release their own subscriptions.",
    "acceptance": "On entering view the shell builds from the base upward over about two seconds and holds, then turns slowly. Density is even everywhere: the small finial and the large knot carry the same particles-per-square-millimetre, with no dense speckle on the small parts. Particles nearer the camera are visibly larger than those on the far side, and none of them pop out of existence as the cloud turns. The rings hug the silhouette — they widen where the form widens — and do not cut through the middle of it. Resize the container from wide to narrow: the shell stays fully in frame and particles keep the same apparent size rather than growing or shrinking. With reduced motion a fully assembled three-quarter view renders immediately and holds still.",
    "criticalRegion": "critical"
